    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nIsraeli AIR Autonomous Cargo Aircraft Set to Start Flight Tests in Florida\n8 photos\nPhoto: AIR VEV\nAIR VEV, the Israel-based maker of the famous Air One personal eVTOL (electric vertical takeoff and landing), has officially sent its first Air 120D cargo aircraft to its Florida headquarters, for flight demonstrations. This autonomous cargo version of the popular Air One shows promising potential for the US Air Force and beyond.\nAIR VEV is particularly known for Air One, the exciting two-seat personal aircraft currently working to secure the FAA (Federal Aviation Administration) Light Sport Aircraft certification, but the Israeli company also makes an autonomous cargo version, the Air 120D, with excellent capabilities for defense and contested logistics, in addition to commercial cargo and humanitarian missions.\nA few months ago, AIR had officially announced a major partnership with FDOT (Florida Department of Transportation) that would allow it to start manufacturing its eVTOLs in the US. FDOT named AIR as the first company that will produce and operate eVTOLs in Florida under the SunTrax umbrella, one of the most important and ambitious AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) initiatives in the US so far.\nSunTrax is meant to operate as a special campus for testing both air and ground advanced mobility solutions, via the SunTrax Ground and SunTrax Air divisions. As a result, AIR has established its new US headquarters here, at the Orlando-based SunTrax hub, confirming plans to kick off flight plans for both the Air One and Air 120D here.\nThe arrival of the first Air 120D aircraft marks the first step in this large-scale plan aiming for commercial production in the near future. According to the Israeli company, its autonomous cargo aircraft falls under the US Department of War Group 4 UAS category, which makes it compatible with defense logistics in contested environments and environments with little to no infrastructure.\nAs a completely autonomous aircraft, the Air 120D eliminates risks for human personnel, while accessing remote areas thanks to its vertical takeoff and landing capabilities that don’t require conventional airstrips or infrastructure. These capabilities also make it compatible with maritime logistics and humanitarian aid. In terms of cargo capacity, the Air 120 D is designed to carry around 550 pounds in a 70-cubic-foot cargo bay.\nThis past summer, AIR has also officially unveiled the popular Air One personal eVTOL at the 2026 EEA AirVenture event in Oshkosh. The Israeli company was actively involved in developing the MOSAIC regulatory framework alongside FAA, ASTM (American Society for Testing and Materials), and civil aviation authorities from other countries.\nThis new legislation is said to bridge the gap between light-category aircraft (the existing regulatory framework for aircraft like the Air One), and new-generation eVTOL aircraft.\nAs it prepares for flight demonstrations and commercial production in Florida, AIR is also constantly increasing its order backlog, with more than $1 billion currently estimated forpre-orders across its entire eVTOL portfolio.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAIR VEV AIR 120D Air autonomous cargo drone autonomous cargo aircraft autonomous aircraft\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT 2027 Hyundai Elantra & 2028 Elantra Hybrid: How Do They Compare to Other Affordable Compact Sedans?",
